    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d324">

                <pb facs="0487" n="375" xml:id="pg375"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7394</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cunningham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d324-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">[No.] 8936</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-23">October 23, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to this office’s
                    despatch No. 8834 of September 4, 1935,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d324-f2"/> in
                    which was reported the informal protest of Mr. Itaro Ishii, Japanese Consul
                    General, in regard to articles published recently in the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >China Weekly Review</hi><anchor n="13" xml:id="d324-f3"/> (American and
                    Chinese registered), and to enclose a copy of a memorandum of
                        conversation<anchor n="12" xml:id="d324-f4"/> between Consul General Ishii
                    and myself on October 18, 1935.</p>
                <p>Consul General Ishii referred to his previous visit on September 3, 1935, and
                    stated that it was more or less informal and unofficial but that since no relief
                    had been obtained by his oral protest it would be necessary for him to make an
                    official protest in writing against the publication in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">China Weekly Review</hi> of the articles mentioned in
                    the enclosure to my despatch referred to above. I informed Mr. Ishii that it
                    would be impossible for me to comply with his request and apologize for the
                    publication of the articles in the <hi rendition="#italic">China Weekly
                        Review</hi>, nor would it be possible for me to censor Mr. Powell<anchor
                        n="14" xml:id="d324-f5"/> for publishing the articles.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edwin S.
                    Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d324-f1" n="11" type="source" xml:id="d324-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Consul General in his despatch No.
                    10393, October 23; received November 18.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d324-f2" n="12" xml:id="d324-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d324-f3" n="13" xml:id="d324-f3-content">
                <p>The articles in question referred to the Emperor of Japan in terms considered
                    objectionable by the Japanese.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d324-f4" n="12" xml:id="d324-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d324-f5" n="14" xml:id="d324-f5-content">
                <p>John B. Powell, American editor and publisher of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >China Weekly Review</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
